**Lemma 5.3 (complete accepted-target binding on the actual route).** On
$\mathcal A_{\mathrm{gc}}$, current checks and all applicable past
predicates succeed for the authentic independently encoded body of the
selected $h_k$. Both general arms bind the same unique actual target as
GCA. SourceOffset includes $t=0$ before any header IN and $t=f-e$ after
the final idle CTL; neither endpoint needs a strict-Reserve predicate.

Proof. GCA's actual CoverRef/PackageRef callbacks acquire $c,p$, check
the entire expected tagged binding and resolve their immutable backing.
Definition 2.1 preserves these routines. The domain supplies an actual
finite completed predecessor, including every earlier suffix. Its
frozen five-field items supply each actual earlier mode and frame at
their original sites. Induction along this finite witness with the
matching literal Step proves GCA Theorem 4.2's correspondence for this
graph; extra converter rows have no new IN. GCA's physical locator
argument and unary range calculation, Theorems 5.5–5.6, then apply.
This argument uses completion of the given predecessor, not completion
of the selecting flight. Section8 proves the latter by induction.
Thus AcquisitionOffset gives $k=a+t$ exactly in $[a,b]$, and SourceOffset
finds its unique completed item and its physical $e,f$ with
$a<e<f<b$, then gives $k=e+t$ exactly in $[e,f]$.

Theorem 4.3 gives the actual prefix from each independent body's basic
data. Its acquisition annotation is the one at $a$. In the source arm
the unique entry is already included at $e$; the exit is included just
when $k=f$. All constructed request and source facts agree because they
are facts of this same call in this same actual predecessor. The old
prefix need not contain its later response or acquired copy. GCA's
current immutable cover, source-item resolution and bounded locator
check those later facts without changing the old prefix. Mode0 is the
existing scoped validation, with this graph's actual clocks and origins.
Consequently no check needs a future payload as a decoder input. ∎

This lemma concerns authentic, currently delivered bodies. A self-consistent
body plus a printed reference is not a hostile-input authentication
theorem. The current channel supplies the binding between that body
and the selected package. Without the actual cover/package resolution
and sealed-copy route, the old-body tests alone do not establish that
a later $f$ or $b$ is genuine. This distinction is part of the service
contract, not an omitted decoder hypothesis asserted as a native bridge.

